\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Tractor-trailer geometry. The tractor rear axle carries the hitch ($L_h \approx 0$), from which the trailer of wheelbase $L_t$ trails at articulation angle $\gamma = \psi - \psi _t$. The front wheel is steered by $\delta $ relative to the tractor heading $\psi $, and $L$ is the tractor wheelbase.\relax }}{14}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces The simulated scene from which the bird's-eye-view observation is cropped. The drivable lane corridor is white and everything outside it is blocked; the tractor is blue and the trailer orange. This top-down view, centered on the vehicle and reduced to a $32 \times 32$ crop, is the image the policy receives.\relax }}{18}{figure.caption.25}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Architecture of the learning system: an observation (engineered state, LiDAR, or BEV image) is encoded into a feature vector and passed to the TD3 actor and critics, which output a steering rate and a stop signal. The reward combines path-tracking and hitch-stability terms.\relax }}{26}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Reverse lane-following completion against training steps for TD3 under each reward formulation. The multiplicative reward reaches the highest and steadiest completion.\relax }}{35}{figure.caption.38}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Observation-space ablation learning curves, completion against training steps for the identical TD3 recipe under each observation. The raw image never leaves zero, and the higher-dimensional LiDAR scans learn less reliably than the compact state.\relax }}{41}{figure.caption.44}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Geometry-distillation training. In Stage 1 a convolutional encoder is trained by supervised regression to predict the vehicle's path geometry from the bird's-eye-view image, using the simulator's true state as a privileged teacher, and is then frozen. In Stage 2 the policy is trained from scratch with TD3 on the frozen geometry prediction concatenated with the steering and hitch angles supplied as proprioception.\relax }}{44}{figure.caption.45}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Geometry-encoder policy completion against training steps on forward lane-following, trained from scratch on the frozen encoder. Completion reaches parity with the engineered-state baseline by about 300 thousand steps with no collapse.\relax }}{48}{figure.caption.48}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Reverse obstacle maneuvering, outcome rates against maximum dynamic difficulty. No crash occurs below the gate near a dynamic difficulty of 0.5, so stopping above it is the correct action.\relax }}{53}{figure.caption.55}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces The physical articulated platform: an AgileX Hunter SE tractor coupled to the lab trailer through a revolute hitch at the tractor rear axle.\relax }}{63}{figure.caption.65}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Deployment software stack. Autoware's localization and perception are retained unchanged, while the planning and control layers (the conventional freespace planner and MPC/LQR tracker) are replaced by the learned RL bridge. The two control paths are mutually exclusive and selected at launch.\relax }}{65}{figure.caption.69}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces RL bridge dataflow. The \texttt {lane\_reference\_node}, the LiDAR-based \texttt {hitch\_angle\_estimator}, and Autoware localization supply the policy observation to \texttt {rl\_bridge\_node}, which queries the TD3 policy at 10\,Hz and emits a control command that \texttt {controller\_canbridge} translates into Hunter SE CAN frames. Vehicle velocity and steering feedback return through the same bridge.\relax }}{67}{figure.caption.70}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces The deployment running in RViz. The left panel shows the occupancy image the policy consumes, drivable in white and blocked in black, built from the lanelet map and the infrastructure obstacle list. The right panel shows the same scene in the map frame, with the tractor and trailer, the LiDAR return field, and the estimated hitch angle annotated on the vehicle.\relax }}{67}{figure.caption.71}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces Tractor tracks for the four deployment trials against the surveyed lane centerline. Solid tracks are forward trials, dashed tracks are reverse. The two forward tracks are nearly coincident and largely hide one another. The forward trials sit on the reference lane throughout, while the reverse trials bow outward by up to two meters before converging near point B.\relax }}{75}{figure.caption.83}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.6}{\ignorespaces The four deployment trials against distance traveled along the reference path. (a) Trailer cross-track error, with the lane corridor shaded. (b) Articulation angle against the operational and jackknife limits. (c) Commanded steering rate against its saturation limit. Solid traces are forward trials, dashed traces are reverse. The reverse trials oscillate continuously and hold the steering rate on its limit, while the forward trials do neither.\relax }}{76}{figure.caption.84}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {B.1}{\ignorespaces Reconstruction of the target distribution-center site in Blender. OpenStreetMap building footprints are extruded to form the 3D building meshes and draped over high-resolution aerial orthoimagery from the ESRI World Imagery basemap, both layers imported through the Blender GIS plugin. The same GIS-driven procedure reconstructs any real distribution center from its geographic coordinates.\relax }}{91}{figure.caption.86}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {B.2}{\ignorespaces The \texttt {electrans} tractor spawned at a loading dock within the world.\relax }}{92}{figure.caption.87}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {B.3}{\ignorespaces The \texttt {electrans} tractor-trailer model. The tractor reuses the AgileX Hunter \texttt {xacro} description (Ackermann front steering, continuous rear wheels, and \texttt {ros2\_control} blocks), rescaled and given a cab-over terminal-tractor body mesh. The box trailer is attached through a revolute hitch joint at the tractor rear axle.\relax }}{93}{figure.caption.88}%
